is dude look at that big spider gift  Livingston Sango never thought he'd be  doing this showing his son artwork done  in the 1940s by his late father  Livingston Sango his son's grandfather  the paintings are part of an exhibition  at the National Gallery in Zimbabwe's  Capital Harare a collection done more  than 70 years ago by young black  students at Cyrene Anglican Mission  School some of this picture I'm singing  for the first time in my life when we  started these exhibitions I don't think  he even remembered them but here we are  I am now seeing my father at the age of  18 when I only remember him as an  ornament  Cyrene Anglican Mission School was the  first school to teach art to black  children in what was then white minority  ruled Rhodesia the most special thing  about this collection is that it went on  a world tour in the 1940s from 1947 to  1953 so the rest of the world saw these  artworks in the 40s and 50s and  absolutely loved it but zimbabweans had  never seen the artworks before after  that tour unsold artworks were stored in  the basement of a church in the UK where  they were forgotten for years in the  1970s they were rediscovered and sold to  an art collector who then sold the  collection to a Zimbabwean  philanthropist in the diaspora the Stars  a bright exhibition is now briefly back  home this is a picture of my father  his name was Claxton William candier  lianda candiero a popular radio  presenter here says it's been amazing  putting together the pieces of his  family's history his father died in  1990. We Are history we are a result of  History this is a history that I'll  share with my children I've already  bought them here anyway  this is a history that I am taking back  to my mom the wife to say did you know  that your husband did this you didn't  know artwork that was sold during the  world tour in the late 1940s and early  1950s helped to fund the school and pay  for scholarships for some of the  students the paintings and drawings  cover a seven year period and represent  just a small selection of more than 600  works from one collection hundreds more  exists in public and private collections  around the world after this exhibition  in Zimbabwe the art will tour abroad  again with calls for the repatriation of  African art to the continent growing  some locals want to see the Cyrene  paintings returned home permanently  haramutasa Al Jazeera Harare
 

